  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread showers and thunderstorms early \nThursday morning and possibly Thursday afternoon \n\nA frontal wave traverses over southern New England late tonight into \ntomorrow morning. A surface warm front lifts northeast with a rich \nof plume of moisture containing PWATs between 1.5 and 2\". This will \nsupport a period of widespread showers with embedded thunder early \ntomorrow morning across southern New England. Shower activity begins \naround 2AM across the western areas and reaches the east coast \naround 4-6AM. Heavy downpours are likely with rich moisture in \nplace. There is a marginal risk for flash flooding across western \nMA/CT in the Springfield/Hartford areas, but there is no strong \nsignal for anything overly impactful at this time. Elevated \ninstability will support some rumbles of thunder as showers move \nacross the region, but deep layer shear and low/mid-level lapse \nrates do not appear to be favorable for deep moist convection \ncapable of producing strong to severe storms.\n\nAs the frontal wave moves offshore, some mid-level dry air intrudes \nfrom the west which should support some clearing in the late-\nmorning to early afternoon hours. With the clearing will come \ndiurnal heating and instability which will introduce another \npotential round of isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms Thursday afternoon. Hi-Res model guidance has been\ninconsistent with afternoon convection Thursday, so we leaned \non a uniform 40% PoP for southern New England after 2PM. Expect \nclearing Thursday night into Friday with warm/sunny conditions \nexpected Friday afternoon.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry & very pleasant Fri into early next week \nexcept for some diurnally scattered showers mainly on Sat. Pleasant \ntemps too with highs in the 70s...but lower-middle 80s Fri. \n\nUpper level shortwave energy will close off over the Canadian \nMaritimes over the Holiday Weekend. This will carve out an upper \ntrough over the northeast resulting in pleasant weather. Mainly dry \nweather expected over the majority of this time. However...some \nnorthern stream shortwave energy will probably result in diurnally \ndriven scattered showers mainly on Sat...but can not rule out a few \non Fri. High pressure building southward from eastern Canada Sun \ninto Mon should bring an end to this risk for the latter half of the \nHoliday Weekend. Highs on Fri will mainly be in the lower to middle \n80s...but generally 70s to near 80 this weekend into early next \nweek. In fact...coolest days will be Sat and Sun as the eastern MA \ncoast/Cape and Islands probably only see highs in the upper 60s to \nthe lower 70s with a stiff northeast afternoon breeze. Overnight \nlows mainly in the 50s to the lower 60s...but there might be some \n40s in the normally coolest outlying locations by Sun night/Mon AM \nas high pressure builds across the region. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Elevated risk for dangerous rip currents for the \nHoliday Weekend especially by Sun into Mon. \n\nGiven persistent NE low level flow and swell...we do expect an \nelevated risk for dangerous rip currents for the Holiday Weekend. \nThis is especially true by Sun into Mon on east facing ocean exposed \nbeaches.",
